= Solution
{parent=1}

The <factor theorem> supplies the factor $x-3$. <Polynomial division> gives
$$
x^3-x^2-8x+6=(x-3)(x^2+2x-2).
$$
The <quadratic formula> then gives the other two <roots of a polynomial>:
$$
\boxed{x=-1+\sqrt3,\quad x=-1-\sqrt3.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Isolate the <square root>: $\sqrt{x+16}=x-4$. A real solution must have $x\geq4$, because a real principal <square root> is nonnegative. Squaring gives $x+16=(x-4)^2$, hence $x(x-9)=0$. The candidate zero violates $x\geq4$; substitution verifies the other candidate. Thus
$$
\boxed{x=9.}
$$
The sign restriction prevents the extraneous solution introduced by squaring.

