= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <partial fraction decomposition>:
$$
\frac1{x(x-4)}=-\frac1{4x}+\frac1{4(x-4)}.
$$
Integrating the two logarithmic <derivatives> gives
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{dx}{x^2-4x}=\frac14\log\left|\frac{x-4}{x}\right|+C.}
$$
This holds on each interval avoiding the two <poles> $0$ and $4$, with an independent integration constant on each interval.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <change of variables> $u=x^2$, $du=2x\,dx$, gives
$$
\boxed{\int xe^{x^2}\,dx=\frac12e^{x^2}+C.}
$$
Differentiation verifies the <antiderivative> for every real $x$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The double-angle identity $\cos(2x)=2\cos^2x-1$ reduces the equation to $\cos x(1+2\cos x)=0$. On the specified interval the <cosine> takes each of the values $0$ and $-1/2$ once, giving
$$
\boxed{x=\frac\pi2,\quad x=\frac{2\pi}3.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
