Eliminating the parameter gives $x^2+y^2/4=1$, an <ellipse> centered at the origin with horizontal semiaxis one and vertical semiaxis two. At $t=0$ the particle is at $(1,0)$ with <velocity> $(0,2)$, so it travels **counterclockwise**, reaching $(0,2),(-1,0),(0,-2)$ successively and returning after $2\pi$. The right panel of the graph figure shows this direction.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <parametric curve> with $dx/dt\ne0$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{2\cos t}{-\sin t}.
$$
The specified point has $\cos t=\sin t=1/\sqrt2$, hence $t=\pi/4$ in the given traversal. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=-2.}
$$

