= Solution
{parent=i}

An axial section gives similar right triangles. At height $x$ above the base, the cone's remaining height is $h-x$, so the cylinder's radius is $r=(h-x)\tan\alpha$. Therefore its <volume> is
$$
\boxed{V(x)=\pi x(h-x)^2\tan^2\alpha,\qquad0\leq x\leq h.}
$$
Assume $h>0$ and $0<\alpha<\pi/2$ for a nondegenerate cone. The geometry and the dimensionless optimization curves are illustrated below.

\Image[paper-1-cylinder-in-cone.png]
{title=Axial section and different optimal heights for cylinder volume and curved area inside a cone}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At fixed $h,\alpha$, the <derivative> is $V'=\pi\tan^2\alpha\,(h-x)(h-3x)$. It is positive for $0<x<h/3$ and negative for $h/3<x<h$. The endpoint volumes are zero. Thus the unique positive-volume global <maximum> is at
$$
\boxed{x=\frac h3.}
$$
Its value is $4\pi h^3\tan^2\alpha/27$, whereas the cone volume is $\pi h^3\tan^2\alpha/3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{V_{\rm cylinder}}{V_{\rm cone}}=\frac49.}
$$
This is the volume case of the <optimal cylinder inscribed in a cone>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The curved <surface area> excludes the two end disks, so $A=2\pi rx=2\pi\tan\alpha\,x(h-x)$. Completing the square gives
$$
A=2\pi\tan\alpha\left[\frac{h^2}{4}-\left(x-\frac h2\right)^2\right].
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{x=\frac h2,\qquad A_{\max}=\frac{\pi h^2\tan\alpha}{2}.}
$$
It differs from the volume optimum because the objective contains one power of the radius rather than two.

