= Solution
{parent=i}

The <scalar triple product> is the <determinant> of the matrix with columns $a,b,c$. A cyclic permutation of three columns is a product of two swaps, so its determinant sign is positive. Therefore $[a,b,c]=[b,c,a]$. This uses the alternating multilinearity of the <determinant>, and remains true for linearly dependent vectors.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <determinant> defining $[a,a,c]$ has two equal columns. Interchanging them both leaves it unchanged and reverses its sign, so it is zero. Equivalently, the <cross product> $a\times c$ is orthogonal to $a$, giving $a\cdot(a\times c)=0$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Three vectors in real three-dimensional space have zero <determinant> exactly when they are <linearly dependent>. Their <linear span> then has dimension at most two, so they lie in a common plane through the origin. Conversely vectors in such a plane span at most two dimensions, so their <scalar triple product> vanishes. This includes parallel nonzero vectors: they need not determine a unique plane, but they lie in at least one. Even zero vectors satisfy the determinant characterization, though the question excludes them.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By the <vector triple product identity>,
$$
(a\times b)\times(a\times c)=a[(a\times b)\cdot c]-c[(a\times b)\cdot a]=[a,b,c]a.
$$
The last inner product vanishes by perpendicularity. Taking the <inner product> with $d$ gives
$$
[a\times b,a\times c,d]=d\cdot[(a\times b)\times(a\times c)]=(a\cdot d)[a,b,c].
$$
This is the identity for <cross products with a common vector>, and holds without nondegeneracy assumptions.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At an intersection, $a+tu=b+t'v$, so $b-a=tu-t'v$. Alternating multilinearity of the <scalar triple product> then gives $[v,b-a,u]=t[v,u,u]-t'[v,v,u]=0$, establishing $[v,b,u]=[v,a,u]$.

For the location, write $b=a+tu-t'v$ and take its <scalar triple product> with $a$ and $v$:
$$
[a,b,v]=[a,a+tu-t'v,v]=t[a,u,v].
$$
Under the additional stated assumption $[a,u,v]\ne0$, division gives
$$
\boxed{r=a+\frac{[a,b,v]}{[a,u,v]}u.}
$$
These are <intersection parameters from scalar triple products>. The formula presupposes an intersection; the first coplanarity condition by itself also holds for distinct parallel lines. Intersections can exist with the displayed denominator zero, in which case a different auxiliary vector or a component calculation is needed.

= 16
