= Solution
{parent=a}

For $p>0$, <integration by parts> gives the <Gamma function recurrence>:
$$
\Gamma(p+1)=\int_0^\infty x^pe^{-x}dx=[-x^pe^{-x}]_0^\infty+p\int_0^\infty x^{p-1}e^{-x}dx=p\Gamma(p).
$$
The endpoint terms vanish because $x^p\to0$ at zero and the exponential dominates the power at infinity. Also $\Gamma(1)=\int_0^\infty e^{-x}dx=1$. Iterating for nonnegative <integers> $n$ therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\Gamma(n+1)=n!.}
$$
Negative integers are outside this integral definition and factorial conclusion.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $t=\log(1/x)$, so $x=e^{-t}$ and $dx=-e^{-t}dt$. The <improper integral> becomes
$$
\int_0^1[\log(1/x)]^4dx=\int_0^\infty t^4e^{-t}dt=\Gamma(5).
$$
The <Gamma function recurrence> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1[\log(1/x)]^4dx=24.}
$$
The exponential-transformed integrand also proves convergence at the logarithmically singular endpoint.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the <Gamma function> integral at $p=1/2$, use $x=t^2$, $dx=2t\,dt$, with $t>0$. Then $x^{-1/2}dx=2\,dt$, so
$$
\Gamma(1/2)=\int_0^\infty x^{-1/2}e^{-x}dx=2\int_0^\infty e^{-t^2}dt.
$$
The substitution can first be made on finite positive intervals and then extended by monotone limits, justifying the endpoint passage.

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The integrand in the preceding expression is nonnegative. <Tonelli's theorem> permits squaring as a quadrant integral; then <polar coordinates> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Gamma(1/2)^2&=4\int_0^\infty\int_0^\infty e^{-(u^2+v^2)}du\,dv\\
&=4\int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^\infty e^{-r^2}r\,dr\,d\theta=\pi.
\end{aligned}
$$
The defining <integral> is positive, fixing the sign of the square root. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\Gamma(1/2)^2=\pi,\qquad\Gamma(1/2)=\sqrt\pi.}
$$
This is the positive-quadrant proof of the <Gaussian integral>.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
