= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The summand is $\sqrt n/\sqrt{n+1}=\sqrt{n/(n+1)}\to1$, rather than zero. The necessary <term test for divergence> fails. Therefore the positive-term series **diverges to $+\infty$**.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $a_n=n^2/n!$, the <ratio test> gives
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac{(n+1)^2}{n^2(n+1)}=\frac{n+1}{n^2}\longrightarrow0<1.
$$
Thus the series **converges absolutely**. As a check, $n^2=n(n-1)+n$ and the exponential <power series> give the sum $e+e=2e$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $a_n=n^n/n!$, successive terms satisfy
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\left(1+\frac1n\right)^n\longrightarrow e>1.
$$
They eventually grow by a factor strictly greater than one and cannot tend to zero. Hence the series **diverges to $+\infty$**, by the <ratio test> or the <term test for divergence>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Maclaurin series> give $\sin(3x)=3x+O(x^3)$ and $\sinh x=x+O(x^3)$. Dividing by $x$ in a punctured neighborhood of zero gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin(3x)}{\sinh x}=3.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The linear terms cancel: $\tan x=x+x^3/3+O(x^5)$, whereas $\tanh x=x-x^3/3+O(x^5)$. Also $\sinh x-x=x^3/6+O(x^5)$. Their leading nonzero terms therefore give
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\tan x-\tanh x}{\sinh x-x}=\frac{2/3}{1/6}=4.}
$$
The <Maclaurin series> remainder orders justify division after factoring out $x^3$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

To retain the finite term after cancellation, expand far enough before dividing. The <Maclaurin series> are
$$
\csc x=x^{-1}+\frac x6+\frac{7x^3}{360}+O(x^5),\qquad
\sinh x=x+\frac{x^3}{6}+\frac{x^5}{120}+O(x^7).
$$
Thus
$$
\frac{\csc x}{x^3}-\frac{\sinh x}{x^5}
=\left(x^{-4}+\frac{x^{-2}}6+\frac7{360}\right)-\left(x^{-4}+\frac{x^{-2}}6+\frac1{120}\right)+O(x^2),
$$
and hence
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\left(\frac{\csc x}{x^3}-\frac{\sinh x}{x^5}\right)=\frac1{90}.}
$$
